WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:03.510
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:10.260
This is a collaboration between DeepMind and
OpenAI on using human demonstrations to teach

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.510
an AI to play games really well.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:18.410
The basis of this work is reinforcement learning,
which is about choosing a set of actions in

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.270
an environment to maximize a score.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:27.400
For some games, this score is typically provided
by the game itself, but in more complex games,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:33.350
for instance, ones that require exploration,
this score is not too useful to train an AI.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:40.730
In this project, the key idea is to use human
demonstrations to teach an AI how to succeed.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:46.329
This means that we can sit down, play the
game, show the footage to the AI and hope

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:48.970
that it learns something useful from it.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:53.960
Now the most trivial implementation of this
would be to imitate the footage too closely,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.899
or in other words, simply redo what the human
has done.

00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:03.760
That would be a trivial endeavor, and it is
the most common way of misunderstanding what

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.649
is happening here, so I will emphasize that
is not the case.

00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:14.020
Just imitating what the human player does
would not be very useful because one, it puts

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.509
too much burden on the humans, that’s not
what we want, and number two, the AI could

00:01:19.509 --> 00:01:24.719
not be significantly better than the human
demonstrator, that’s also not what we want.

00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:30.439
In fact, if we have a look at the paper, the
first figure shows us right away how badly

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:33.179
a simpler imitation program performs.

00:01:33.179 --> 00:01:35.659
That’s not what this algorithm is doing.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.810
What it does instead is that it looks at the
footage as the human plays the game, and tries

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.560
to guess what they were trying to accomplish.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.709
Then, we can tell a reinforcement learner
that this is now our reward function and it

00:01:48.709 --> 00:01:51.669
should train to become better at that.

00:01:51.669 --> 00:01:57.259
As you see here, it can play an exploration-heavy
game such as Atari “Hero”, and in the

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:01.619
footage here above you see the rewards over
time, the higher the better.

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:06.990
This AI performs really well in this game,
and significantly outperforms reinforcement

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:12.880
learner agents trained from scratch on Montezuma’s
revenge as well, although it can still get

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:22.830
stuck on a ladder.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:28.410
We discussed earlier a curious AI that was
quickly getting bored by ladders and moved

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.520
on to more exciting endeavors in the game.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.700
The performance of the new agent seems roughly
equivalent to an agent trained from scratch

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:42.410
in the game Pong, presumably because of the
lack of exploration and the fact that it is

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:46.480
very easy to understand how to score points
in this game.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.180
But wait, in the previous episode we just
talked about an algorithm where we didn’t

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:57.550
even need to play, we could just sit in our
favorite armchair and direct the algorithm.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.510
So why play?

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:05.850
Well, just providing feedback is clearly very
convenient, but as we can only specify what

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.100
we liked and what we didn’t like, it is
not very efficient.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:14.230
With the human demonstrations here, we can
immediately show the AI what we are looking

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:21.190
for, and, as it is able to learn the principles
and then improve further, and eventually become

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:26.300
better than the human demonstrator, this work
provides a highly desirable alternative to

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.470
already existing techniques Loving it.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:34.430
If you have a look at the paper, you will
also see how the authors incorporated a cool

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:39.490
additional step to the pipeline where we can
add annotations to the training footage, so

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:40.870
make sure to have a look!

